<span class="kicker">2026-09-05 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Keep Your Patio and Driveway From Molding</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>What most people call “mold” growing on a patio or driveway is usually one of a few different organic growths, algae, moss, or mildew, each of which shows up for slightly different reasons but responds to a lot of the same prevention and cleaning approaches. </p>
<p>Whatever you call it, that greenish, blackish, or slippery film building up on a hardscape surface is a moisture and organic buildup problem, and understanding what’s actually feeding it is the key to keeping it from coming back once you’ve cleaned it off.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we field this question fairly often across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, particularly from homeowners with shaded patios or driveways under heavy tree cover. This guide covers what’s actually growing on your hardscape, how to clean it off properly, and the prevention steps that keep it from returning, especially in the shadiest, dampest corners of a property where this problem tends to be worst.</p>
<h2><b>What’s Actually Growing on Your Patio or Driveway</b></h2>
<h3><b>Algae</b></h3>
<p>Algae is the most common culprit behind a greenish film on hardscape surfaces, thriving in consistently damp, shaded conditions. It tends to appear as a slick, slippery coating rather than a raised or fuzzy texture, and it’s often the first organic growth to show up on a new problem area.</p>
<h3><b>Moss</b></h3>
<p>Moss forms a denser, more textured green growth, often in small patches or clumps rather than a uniform film. It favors similar damp, shaded conditions to algae but tends to establish more slowly and holds on more stubbornly once it’s taken hold, since its root-like structures grip into small surface irregularities and joint material.</p>
<h3><b>Mold and Mildew</b></h3>
<p>True mold and mildew are less common outdoors than algae or moss, but they can appear on hardscape surfaces in particularly damp, poorly ventilated areas, often showing as a darker, sometimes black or gray discoloration rather than green. This is more frequently seen on surfaces that stay wet for extended periods with little airflow or direct sun exposure.</p>
<h2><b>Comparing the Common Culprits</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Growth Type</b></td><td><b>Appearance</b></td><td><b>Texture</b></td><td><b>Where It Thrives Most</b></td></tr><tr><td>Algae</td><td>Green film</td><td>Slick, slippery</td><td>Consistently damp, shaded surfaces</td></tr><tr><td>Moss</td><td>Green patches or clumps</td><td>Dense, textured</td><td>Shaded areas with joint material or surface irregularities</td></tr><tr><td>Mold/Mildew</td><td>Dark gray or black discoloration</td><td>Can appear fuzzy or spotted</td><td>Poorly ventilated, persistently wet areas</td></tr></tbody></table><h2><b>Why This Happens: The Root Causes</b></h2>
<h3><b>Persistent Moisture</b></h3>
<p>Every type of growth covered here needs sustained moisture to establish and spread. A surface that dries out reasonably quickly after rain or irrigation is far less hospitable than one that stays damp for extended periods.</p>
<h3><b>Lack of Direct Sunlight</b></h3>
<p>Shade slows evaporation and keeps surfaces damp longer than direct sun exposure would. This is why north-facing patios, areas under dense tree canopy, or spaces enclosed by walls or fencing on multiple sides tend to develop these problems more readily than open, sun-exposed areas.</p>
<h3><b>Poor Drainage</b></h3>
<p>A hardscape surface with inadequate drainage slope holds water longer than one that sheds it properly, giving algae, moss, and mold more time to establish before the surface dries out. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-slope-a-paver-walkway/"> how to slope a paver walkway</a> covers proper drainage pitch that applies equally to patios and helps address this root cause directly.</p>
<h3><b>Organic Debris Buildup</b></h3>
<p>Leaves, pollen, and other organic debris accumulating in joints or on the surface provide both a moisture-holding medium and a food source for these growths, accelerating how quickly they establish and spread.</p>
<h2><b>Cleaning Existing Growth</b></h2>
<h3><b>Starting With a Basic Wash</b></h3>
<p>For light algae or early-stage growth, a thorough scrub with a stiff-bristled brush and a mixture of water and mild dish soap, followed by a rinse, is often enough to remove the surface growth and temporarily halt its progression.</p>
<h3><b>Using a Pressure Washer</b></h3>
<p>For more established growth, a pressure washer set to an appropriate pressure for your specific paver or concrete material removes algae and moss more thoroughly and quickly than manual scrubbing alone. It’s worth being cautious with pressure setting on softer or more textured paver surfaces, since excessive pressure can damage the surface or blast out joint sand along with the growth you’re trying to remove.</p>
<h3><b>Using a Vinegar or Oxygen Bleach Solution</b></h3>
<p>A solution of white vinegar and water, or an oxygen bleach product mixed according to label instructions, offers a more targeted treatment for algae and mold specifically, breaking down the organic material more effectively than plain water and soap alone. Apply, let sit for the recommended time, then scrub and rinse thoroughly.</p>
<h3><b>Commercial Algae and Moss Removers</b></h3>
<p>Purpose-made outdoor algae and moss removal products are widely available and generally formulated to be safe for use on concrete and paver surfaces, offering a more concentrated treatment option than household alternatives for particularly stubborn growth.</p>
<h3><b>A Note on Chlorine Bleach</b></h3>
<p>Standard chlorine bleach can remove algae and mold effectively, but it carries more risk to surrounding plants and can affect certain paver colors or finishes over repeated use. If you choose this route, dilute appropriately, protect nearby plantings, and test on an inconspicuous area first.</p>
<h2><b>Preventing Regrowth After Cleaning</b></h2>
<h3><b>Improving Surface Drainage</b></h3>
<p>If standing water or slow drainage is contributing to the problem, addressing the underlying slope issue is a more permanent fix than repeated cleaning alone. This might mean regrading a section of patio or walkway, or in more significant cases, adding a drain to manage water that’s collecting in a persistent low spot.</p>
<h3><b>Increasing Sunlight Exposure Where Possible</b></h3>
<p>Trimming back overhanging tree branches or dense shrubs that keep a specific area in near-constant shade can meaningfully reduce how quickly algae and moss reestablish, since more direct sun exposure speeds up drying time after rain or irrigation.</p>
<h3><b>Sealing the Surface</b></h3>
<p>A quality paver or concrete sealer creates a less hospitable surface for algae and moss to establish roots into, and it also makes future cleaning easier, since a sealed surface tends to release organic growth more readily than an unsealed, more porous one. Resealing every two to three years maintains this protective benefit over time.</p>
<h3><b>Regular Debris Removal</b></h3>
<p>Sweeping leaves, pollen, and other organic debris off the surface regularly, particularly during fall and spring when this kind of debris is heaviest, reduces the food source and moisture-holding material that feeds algae, moss, and mold growth.</p>
<h3><b>Improving Airflow in Enclosed Areas</b></h3>
<p>For patios enclosed by fencing, walls, or dense landscaping on multiple sides, improving airflow, whether through strategic plant trimming or adjusting fence panel spacing where possible, helps the surface dry out faster after moisture events, directly addressing one of the core conditions these growths need to thrive.</p>

